# Blocks starting a jest run while another one is already in flight.
#
# NOT once-per-session (like 10-jest-pipe, unlike the skill nudges): a second
# concurrent run does not produce a worse result, it produces a WRONG one, and
# making it spendable by trying twice would defeat it.
#
# Why, measured 2026-08-13 on this 16-core machine, tree clean at 1a66f939:
#
#   one full suite at a time   10 runs, 0 failed suites
#   two full suites at once     6 runs, 6 failed (4-6 suites each)
#
# The concurrent failures are all starvation, not behaviour: "Exceeded timeout of
# 10000 ms" in inExtensionMcpServer, mcpConfigWriter, extension-context,
# executor-*ComponentLoading, and a wall-clock assertion in processCleanup.timeout
# reading up to 12,793 ms against a 2,000 ms bound. Every one passes solo.
#
# This is the whole content of .rptc/complete/2026-08-13-jest-full-suite-timeout-flake.md:
# the suspects there were maxWorkers and workerIdleMemoryLimit, and both are
# innocent — 75% workers is green 10/10 when it has the box to itself. What was
# never true is that a gate run had the box to itself. Two Claude sessions work
# this repo (the sibling worktree symlinks node_modules straight back here), so
# overlapping runs are routine and nothing announced them.
#
# Detection: the parent process of any run is `node .../node_modules/.bin/jest`.
# Verified disjoint from its own workers (1 parent vs 12 `jest-worker` children,
# zero overlap), so a match count IS the number of live runs. Watch mode is
# excluded — a resident `--watch` parent is idle almost always, and blocking every
# run for the lifetime of an open watcher would make this unusable.
#
# A jest run that is this hook's OWN ANCESTOR is never counted. Without that, the
# rule fires throughout `tests/hooks/router.test.ts` — the suite exercising the
# router is itself hosted by a `node_modules/.bin/jest` parent — and every guard
# under test would come back blocked by this one. "Do not count the run you are
# executing inside" is also just correct: it is not a competing run.
#
# DBV_JEST_PS overrides the snapshot with a file in `ps -Ao pid=,command=` format.
# It exists so the blocking path can be tested without a second real suite, and
# it is a convenience, not a boundary — anything able to set it could equally run
# jest directly.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER. A PreToolUse hook only sees Claude's own tool calls.
# A run started from a terminal, from inside a shell script, or by a session whose
# checkout predates this file is invisible to it and will contend anyway. That is
# not theoretical: the full-suite verification run for this very change came back
# with 7 failed suites — the exact contention set — and an immediately following
# run, sampled with `ps` throughout to confirm it was alone, was 996/996 green.
# Nothing was sampled during the failing run, so the contention is inferred from
# the signature rather than proven, but the guard plainly did not prevent it.
#
# So: this narrows the window, it does not close it. If a run comes back with
# failures concentrated in inExtensionMcpServer / mcpConfigWriter / extension-context
# / executor-*ComponentLoading, suspect contention BEFORE suspecting your change,
# and re-run while sampling `ps` before you believe either result.
#
# FAILS OPEN: if `ps` is unavailable or returns nothing parseable, the run proceeds.
rule_id=jest-concurrent
rule_once=0

# PIDs from here up to init, space-padded for substring matching.
_jc_ancestors() {
    local p=$$ out=" "
    while [ -n "$p" ] && [ "$p" -gt 1 ] 2>/dev/null; do
        out="$out$p "
        p=$(ps -o ppid= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')
    done
    printf '%s' "$out"
}

# True when some pipeline segment RUNS jest, rather than merely naming it.
#
# The first cut of this matched `jest` as a word anywhere in the command, which
# fired on `ps -Ao pid=,command= | grep jest` — an inspection command that starts
# nothing, and very close to the one this rule's own message recommends. A peer
# session hit it within minutes. Mentioning jest is not running jest, so the test
# is now on the COMMAND WORD of each segment, after stripping env assignments.
_jc_invokes_jest() {
    local norm="$1" seg first rest
    norm="${norm//&&/$'\n'}"
    norm="${norm//||/$'\n'}"
    norm="${norm//;/$'\n'}"
    norm="${norm//|/$'\n'}"

    while IFS= read -r seg; do
        seg="${seg#"${seg%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
        # Drop leading VAR=value prefixes so `TMPDIR=/x npx jest` still counts.
        while [ -n "$seg" ] && [[ "$seg" == [A-Za-z_]*=* ]] && [[ "$seg" == *[[:space:]]* ]]; do
            case "${seg%%[[:space:]]*}" in
                *=*) seg="${seg#*[[:space:]]}"; seg="${seg#"${seg%%[![:space:]]*}"}" ;;
                *) break ;;
            esac
        done
        first="${seg%%[[:space:]]*}"
        case "$seg" in *[[:space:]]*) rest="${seg#*[[:space:]]}" ;; *) rest="" ;; esac

        case "$first" in
            jest|*/jest) return 0 ;;
            npx|pnpm|yarn) case " $rest " in *" jest "*) return 0 ;; esac ;;
            npm) case "$rest" in test|test\ *|run\ test*) return 0 ;; esac ;;
            node|*/node) case "$rest" in *node_modules/.bin/jest*) return 0 ;; esac ;;
        esac
    done <<<"$norm"

    return 1
}

rule_match() {
    [ "$1" = "Bash" ] || return 1

    # Covers `npx jest`, the `node --max-old-space-size=... node_modules/.bin/jest`
    # form in package.json, and the `npm test` / `npm run test:*` wrappers.
    _jc_invokes_jest "$2" || return 1

    local snapshot
    if [ -n "$DBV_JEST_PS" ] && [ -f "$DBV_JEST_PS" ]; then
        snapshot=$(cat "$DBV_JEST_PS" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    else
        snapshot=$(ps -Ao pid=,command= 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    fi
    [ -n "$snapshot" ] || return 1

    local ancestors live=0 pid rest
    ancestors=$(_jc_ancestors)

    # Counted in the shell rather than with `grep -c` so the count is a value to
    # assert on, never an exit status — a check that did not run must not read
    # as "found nothing".
    while read -r pid rest; do
        case "$rest" in *node_modules/.bin/jest*) ;; *) continue ;; esac
        case "$rest" in *--watch*) continue ;; esac
        case "$ancestors" in *" $pid "*) continue ;; esac
        live=$((live + 1))
    done <<<"$snapshot"

    [ "$live" -gt 0 ] || return 1

    JEST_CONCURRENT_COUNT="$live"
    return 0
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<MSG
Blocked: ${JEST_CONCURRENT_COUNT:-another} jest run is already in flight, and results from a
concurrent run are not trustworthy — measured 2026-08-13, two overlapping full
suites failed 4-6 suites every time on 10s timeouts while a solo run failed none
in 10 attempts. A green result would be luck and a red one would be noise.

Wait for it to finish, then re-run. To see what is holding it:

  ps -Ao pid=,lstart=,command= > /tmp/jest-live.txt   # then read that file

If that names a stale parent nothing is waiting on (this machine had an 8-day-old
orphan on 2026-08-13), kill that PID and re-run. Do not work around this by
scoping the run down — a scoped run contends for the same cores.

Background: .rptc/complete/2026-08-13-jest-full-suite-timeout-flake.md
MSG
}
